To automatically prefix a string with a regular expression pattern, you can use a simple function that takes the pattern and the string as input. The function will prepend the pattern to the string and return the resulting string. Here's an example:
```javascript
function autoPrefix(pattern, str) {
  return pattern + str;
}
// Usage
const pattern = /^https:\/\/www\./;
const str = "example.com";
const result = autoPrefix(pattern, str);
console.log(result); // Output: https://www.example.com
```
In this example, the `autoPrefix` function takes two arguments: `pattern` and `str`. It concatenates the pattern and the string using the `+` operator and returns the resulting string. You can call this function with any regular expression pattern and a string, and it will automatically prefix the string with the pattern.

